# ik_llama.cpp / llama.cpp tools default to writing a log file into the
# current working directory (confirmed: llama-imatrix --help does this)
llama.log

# Vendored source. vendor/heretic is a tracked git submodule (see
# .gitmodules and PROVENANCE.md Sec. 5) - reference-only copy of heretic's
# own source for cross-referencing (e.g. codegraph indexing); the pipeline
# itself still runs the pip-installed heretic-llm package (requirements.txt),
# never code from this directory. Anything else dropped under vendor/ ad hoc
# (not registered as a submodule) stays ignored.
vendor/*
!vendor/heretic

# Heretic / model artifacts and outputs (large binary files - keep repo light)
*.safetensors
*.bin
*.gguf
*.pt
*.pth
models/
outputs/
calibration-images/
calibration-images.tmp/
calibration-text.txt
calibration-text.txt.tmp
ik_llama.cpp/
checkpoints/
*.png
*.gif
!docs/**/*.png

# Reference paper (make paper). Fetched on demand and NEVER redistributed:
# the arXiv record uses arXiv's nonexclusive-distrib/1.0 license, which does
# not grant us redistribution rights (see PROVENANCE.md). The PDF is a
# transient local artifact; its <name>.pdf.provenance.json sidecar IS tracked
# as the chain-of-custody record (title/authors/license, pinned version,
# source URL, SHA-256).
references/*.pdf
references/*.pdf.tmp

# Chain-of-custody / audit artifacts: requirements-lock.txt,
# third_party_licenses.json, and the root-level *.provenance.json sidecars
# (calibration-images.provenance.json, calibration-text.txt.provenance.json)
# are intentionally TRACKED, not ignored -- see PROVENANCE.md and
# THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md. Only their transient .tmp write targets are
# ignored here.
*.provenance.json.tmp
requirements-lock.txt.tmp
third_party_licenses.json.tmp
